No, service-492310803820@gcp-sa-aiplatform-re.iam.gserviceaccount.com is not the identity attempting to access the resource.

Here is the technical explanation of why checking this specific service account's access will not solve the issue, and how to verify the correct one:

1. The Security Boundary (Service Agent vs. User Code)
service-492310803820@gcp-sa-aiplatform-re.iam.gserviceaccount.com is the Google-managed Vertex AI Reasoning Engine Service Agent.
Because it is a system-level Service Agent, Google Cloud strictly forbids user-provided Python code (your agent) from running as this identity. This is a security boundary to prevent user code from gaining full control over Google's background infrastructure.
Therefore, even though this service agent does have permission to manage reasoning engines, it is never the account executing the API requests made by your Python code (a2a_planner_tool.py).
2. The Actual Caller: The Runtime Service Account
The account making the GET request from your container is your Runtime Service Account.
If you did not specify a custom service account during deployment, this is always the Compute Engine default service account:

Runtime Account: 492310803820-compute@developer.gserviceaccount.com


In favour of the service agent being the runtime identity

  - The ReasoningEngine spec's effective_identity field is documented as "the identity to use for the Reasoning Engine", and it enumerates the
  possible forms. The first is service-{project}@gcp-sa-aiplatform-re.googleapis.com — explicitly labelled "for SERVICE_AGENT identity type".
  The <something>-compute@developer.gserviceaccount.com form is listed under a different type, SERVICE_ACCOUNT — meaning it would be reported
  differently, and only if you had configured one.
  - Your engines report the SERVICE_AGENT form. So the effective identity in the spec is the -re P4SA.
  - I searched the whole SDK for <NUMBER>-compute@developer.gserviceaccount.com, and it appears only in pipeline-job and prompt-optimizer
  configs (service_account_project_number), plus a Vertex Pipelines workaround — not in ReasoningEngine identity fields. So "the default is
  always the compute default SA" holds for pipelines, which is a different surface.
  - Your own AGENT_RUNTIME_A2A.md tells you to grant roles/agentregistry.viewer to
  service-<PROJECT_NUMBER>@gcp-sa-aiplatform-re.iam.gserviceaccount.com so the planner can resolve its MCP server at runtime. That instruction
  only works if that account is the container's identity.

  Where the err file has a real point

  The distinction it's reaching for exists, just with different principals: Google's deploy docs say the Agent Platform Service Agent —
  service-<PROJECT_NUMBER>@gcp-sa-aiplatform.iam.gserviceaccount.com, note no -re — is used to fetch secrets at deployment time, while "your 
  configured agent identity is used at runtime." So there genuinely are two accounts in play; they're just not the two the file names. Its
  "security boundary" claim is also stated without a source, and it doesn't account for Agent Runtime being a managed-container platform where
  your code is deliberately run on Google's infrastructure.

  I'm not certain enough to have you act on it — and neither should the file convince you. Two ways to settle it definitively:

  # 1. Who actually got denied? The audit log names the principal.
    gcloud logging read \
      'protoPayload.serviceName="aiplatform.googleapis.com" AND protoPayload.status.code=7' \
      --project=labs-gcp-msls-16495-1782829337 --limit=10 \
      --format='value(timestamp, protoPayload.methodName, protoPayload.authenticationInfo.principalEmail)'

  code 7 is PERMISSION_DENIED, and principalEmail is the answer — no interpretation needed. (If Admin Activity logging doesn't surface it, the
  fallback is option 2.)

  # 2. Ask the container directly, in a2a_planner_tool._client()
    import google.auth
    creds, _ = google.auth.default()
    print("runtime identity:", getattr(creds, "service_account_email", None))

  That prints what ADC actually resolves to inside the supervisor — the precise thing in dispute. It's also worth keeping permanently as a
  startup log line, because this whole class of problem is invisible otherwise.

  This matters practically: granting roles to the wrong principal is the most common way to spend an afternoon on an IAM problem that was never
  IAM. Given we already know the office supervisor is in us-east1 and the office planner landed in us-central1, I'd still check the region and
  the PLACEHOLDER engine id before adding any grant at all.